{"t":1791963406,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"327910e4-951a-4943-9c62-eeb250ff3bef","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-10-14T00:47:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 140047\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n847 PM EDT Tue Oct 13 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nCoastal Flood Advisory has been issued for City of Alexandria.\nNo other changes were made.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Near record warmth and mainly dry conditions continue\n  through Wednesday. \n\n- 2) A strong cold front moves through on Friday, bringing \nstrong winds, the potential for thunderstorms, and then cold \nconditions in its wake for this weekend.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Near record warmth and mainly dry conditions continue\nthrough Wednesday.\n\nWeak high pressure nearby and mid-level ridging will continue to \ndominate the forecast heading into Wednesday. The high itself will \nslide offshore later today allowing the mid-level ridge to buckle \nheading into Wednesday. Meanwhile, a series of shortwave \ndisturbances will quickly slide from west to east across northern \nportions of the forecast region as a cold front drops south from the \nGreat Lakes and Ohio River Valley. This front will touch off showers \nand a few thunderstorms late Wednesday afternoon and evening, mainly \nin areas along and north of I-66/US-50 and US-48. A Marginal Risk \n(Level 1 out of 5) for severe weather remains in place for portions \nof western MD, eastern WV, and the VA Highlands Wednesday afternoon \nwhere better instability/shear will reside. Thunderstorm coverage \noverall will be isolated to scattered with damaging winds as the \nprimary concern (especially west of the Blue Ridge (between 3-8pm)). \nMost locations will remain dry through Wednesday afternoon given the \ntiming of the front and residual dry air to punch through as the mid-\nlevel ridge slowly buckles. \n\nBefore the front arrives, expect near record warmth this afternoon \nand again Wednesday. Highs today will reach into the low to mid 80s \n(mid 70s mountains). Lows tonight will fall back into the upper 50s \nand mid 60s. Highs Wednesday will be much warmer in the mid to upper \n80s and potentially low 90s for areas south and east of DC (mid 70s \nmountains).\n\nCooler conditions return briefly Thursday as more of a wedge like \npattern settles in. This is due to a large surface high pressure \nsystem anchored over southern Ontario wedging south along the \neastern face of the Appalachians. Expect cloudy conditions to \nprevail along with passing showers, especially across the central \nand southern 2/3rds of the forecast region. Highs will return closer \nto normal with most locations in the mid to upper 60s. The CAD will \nquickly erode Thursday night into Friday as winds switch back \nto the south/southwest ahead of the next front heading into the \nweekend. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...A strong cold front moves through on Friday, bringing \nstrong winds, the potential for thunderstorms, and then cold \nconditions in its wake for this weekend.\n\nA potent shortwave trough will approach from the Great Lakes on \nFriday. A strong area of low pressure will form just downstream of \nthis trough and track northeastward into the St. Lawrence Valley \nover the course of the day. Southerly flow ahead of the system will \nadvect an unseasonably warm and humid airmass northward into the \narea, with temperatures forecast to climb into the 80s and dewpoints \nreaching into the 60s. The system's cold front will move through the \narea Friday afternoon into Friday evening. The front will move into \nan environment with around 500-1000 J/kg of instability, so a line \nof thunderstorms may ultimately form along the front. If \nthunderstorms were to form, they may be capable of producing \ndamaging winds, given the strong winds that will be in place just \nabove the surface (around 40-45 knots at 850 hPa). Regardless of \nwhether storms form or not, strong northwesterly winds are expected \nbehind the system's cold front late Friday through Friday night and \ninto early Saturday morning. Frequent gusts of 30-40 knots appear \nlikely across the area in northwesterly flow, and some locations may \neven see some gusts above 40 knots. The Alleghenies and Blue Ridge \nappear to be the most likely locations to reach Wind Advisory \ncriteria of 40 knots.\n\nStrong cold advection will also accompany the gusty winds, with 850 \nhPa temperatures plunging from in excess of 15C Friday morning to \nbelow 0C by daybreak Saturday. Some upslope rain showers appear \nlikely Friday night in the Alleghenies, and some snowflakes may even \nmix in by late Friday night. Air temperatures are forecast to dip \ninto the 40s for most Friday night, with 30s in the mountains. By \ndaybreak Saturday, wind chills will be in the 30s at lower \nelevations, and 20s in the mountains. Northwesterly winds will \ngradually decrease through the day Saturday, with temperatures \nforecast to climb into the 50s (40s mountains). High pressure will \nbuild overhead Saturday night, resulting in clear skies and calm \nwinds within what will already be a cool background environment. \nThis will be a near ideal situation for radiational cooling. With \ndewpoints forecast to be in the 20s and lower 30s, it appears as \nthough a freeze may be possible for many locations, especially to \nthe west of the Blue Ridge. At least frost appears likely just about \neverywhere, and freezing temperatures could encompass the entire \narea outside of the immediate downtown areas in DC/Baltimore if \ncooler solutions were to verify. Frost/Freeze headlines will likely \neventually be needed for much of the forecast area. \n\nQuiet weather conditions and a gradual warming trend in temperatures \nare expected for Sunday and Monday as high pressure moves offshore. \nHigh on Sunday and Monday will generally be in the 60s (50s \nmountains), with overnight lows in the 30s and 40s.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /00Z WEDNESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nVFR conditions expected this afternoon through Wednesday afternoon \nas weak high pressure resides nearby. A front will drop into the \narea from the north late Wednesday afternoon and evening bringing \nwith it scattered showers and thunderstorms. This will lead to brief \nsub-VFR reductions between 20-03z/4-11pm. Overall confidence for \nthunderstorm activity over the I-95 corridor terminals is less than \n25 percent with higher thunderstorm probabilities of 25 percent or \ngreater mainly west of MRB. The front drops south of the region \nThursday before stalling in the vicinity of southeast and southwest \nVA. This will put us in more of a wedge-like pattern with on and off \nshowers and widespread sub-VFR (cigs) conditions at most of the \nterminals. Winds will remain out of the south today at 5 to 10 kts \nwith occasional gusts up to 20 kts. South winds continue tonight \ninto Wednesday at less than 10 kts. Did mention LLWS along and west \nof MRB and SHD tonight 2KFT AGL at 35 kts. Winds will switch to the \nnorth and northeast Thursday at less than 15 kts in the wake of the \nfrontal boundary. \n\nVFR conditions and gusty south to southwesterly winds are expected \nFriday morning. A strong cold front will move through Friday \nafternoon. A line of thunderstorms could potentially accompany the \nfront, leading to a brief period of sub-VFR conditions. Strong \nnorthwesterly winds are expected behind the front late Friday \nafternoon through Saturday morning, with gusts of 30-40 knots \npossible at times. Continued VFR conditions are expected on \nSaturday, with winds remaining out of the northwest, but gradually \ndecreasing in magnitude throughout the day.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nSoutherly channeling effects could bring gusts close to SCA \nlevels (around 20 knots), but this likely won't be for a long \nenough period to require a Small Craft Advisory. Instead, Marine\nWeather Statements are more likely to be issued for small \ncrafts to exercise caution. \n\nWinds diminish tonight into Wednesday, with generally sub-SCA \nconditions. A front moves south across the waters Thursday morning, \nand this is likely to bring SCA conditions with gusts around 20 \nknots for several hours. Winds do quickly diminish Thursday \nafternoon.\n\nSCA level winds appear likely in south to southwesterly flow Friday \nmorning. A strong cold front will move through Friday afternoon into \nFriday evening. A brief line of thunderstorms may accompany the \nfront. Winds shift to northwesterly behind the front, and Gale \nconditions appear likely along/behind the front Friday evening into \nearly Saturday morning. Winds will remain northwesterly on Saturday, \nbut gradually decrease in magnitude through the day.\n\n&&\n\n.TIDES/COASTAL FLOODING...\nSouth winds on Wednesday could result in elevated water levels at \nsensitive locations along the Chesapeake Bay. This could result in \nseveral stations reaching Action Stage, with Annapolis and Havre\nde Grace forecast to be closer to minor tidal flooding during \nthe Wednesday morning high tide. A surge of offshore (north) \nwinds Thursday morning will bring water levels back down.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...Coastal Flood Advisory until 10 PM EDT this evening for MDZ014.\nVA...Coastal Flood Advisory until 1 AM EDT Wednesday for VAZ054.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...None.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LFR/KJP/EST\nAVIATION...LFR/KJP/EST\nMARINE...LFR/KJP/KRR\n"},"forecast":{"t":1791963372,"days":{"2026-10-14":{"hi":88,"lo":60,"pop":26,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-15":{"hi":66,"lo":58,"pop":77,"short":"Rain Showers"},"2026-10-16":{"hi":82,"lo":48,"pop":61,"short":"Rain Showers Likely"},"2026-10-17":{"hi":56,"lo":39,"pop":8,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-18":{"hi":61,"lo":42,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-10-19":{"hi":65,"lo":48,"pop":